  "_aboutToast": "May an Undo / ask toast act? (12.1.17 Stage B, the review's #2). Every app keeps, per server-muted identity, the moderator who muted it and a REVISION: a number the app never hands out twice. nextMuteEpisode(prev{by,rev}|null, action, by, fresh): 'unmute' -> null (no mute on record); 'mute' -> prev unchanged when prev exists and prev.by == by (the same moderator's mute repeated or re-announced), else {by, rev: fresh} (a new mute, or a mute by someone else). The SENDER identity of a packet is 'by' (my own mutes: my own identity); someone who leaves the call takes their mute record with them (gone like an unmute; a re-enforced mute after a rejoin is a new revision). Each revision is read in the same step that records the mute (no wait in between). A toast whose button unmutes someone keeps the revisions it acts on: the moderator's Undo, those of the mutes it just made (each read as it was recorded); an ask's Unmute, those of the asker's devices when the ask ARRIVED (or, held, when the asker's mute record arrived). When pressed: toastMayAct(sameCall, revs[{at, now}]) where revs = every kept identity (at = its kept revision) plus every identity the press would act on NOW that it didn't keep (at = null: a device that joined or was muted since); now = that identity's revision at the press (null = not muted). Checked in this order: not the call the toast came from -> stale:call; any identity muted now under a revision other than at (a null at included) -> stale:rev (a newer mute: never lifted by an old toast); no identity still muted under its kept revision -> stale:not-muted (an empty list too); else act. Only 'act' unmutes (through the server's moderation API, which checks rank again). A stale press does nothing but close the toast and say why (wording toastStale*). muteScenarios: start with no record and fresh = 1, 2, 3, ... for the events in order; the toast is made after the first toastAfter events (at = the revision then, if muted) and pressed after all of them.",
